{
  "text_input": "Patient is a 58-year-old male with a history of chronic decompensated liver disease (CLD) for 4 years, type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease (CKD). He presented with a 3-day history of generalized weakness, fatigue, and increased daytime somnolence. Recent ascetic tapping was CBNAAT positive, leading to a diagnosis of abdominal tuberculosis, for which he is on anti-tubercular therapy (ATT). Physical examination revealed a distended abdomen with shifting dullness and flapping tremors, consistent with hepatic encephalopathy. Laboratory investigations showed anemia (Hb 8.6 g/dL), thrombocytopenia (Platelets 50,000/µL), and renal dysfunction (Creatinine 2.3 mg/dL, Urea 53 mg/dL). LFTs showed hypoalbuminemia (Albumin 2.7 g/dL). He was treated for hepatic encephalopathy and abdominal TB with antibiotics (Taxim, Rifagut), insulin, and diuretics. He showed clinical improvement and was discharged on ATT and supportive care.",
